  <section class="page-wrap page-heading"><p class="eyebrow">A WILDER WAY OF THINKING</p><h1>Why beavers <em>matter.</em></h1><p>When beavers change a stream, the effects can reach far beyond the water's edge.</p></section>
  <section class="page-wrap about-layout"><div class="about-art"><span>🌳</span><span>💧</span><span>🦫</span><b>MAKE ROOM<br>FOR WILDLIFE</b></div><div class="about-copy"><article><span class="step">01</span><div><h2>Wetland habitats</h2><p>Beaver ponds and wetlands can provide food, shelter, and breeding areas for fish, amphibians, birds, insects, and plants.</p></div></article><article><span class="step">02</span><div><h2>Water on the landscape</h2><p>Dams may slow the movement of water and help it spread into nearby areas. The results depend on local conditions and the stream.</p></div></article><article><span class="step">03</span><div><h2>A natural balance</h2><p>Beavers can benefit ecosystems, though their activity sometimes conflicts with roads, farmland, or managed waterways. Good wildlife management considers both people and habitats.</p></div></article></div></section>
